Moth can usually work out a value's type from its initializer.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    enabled = true
    count = 42
    ratio = 0.5
    letter = '🦋'
    name = "Moth"
]

You can write a type when it helps the reader or gives the compiler needed
context:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count I32 = 42
    ratio F64 = 0.5
    name String = "Moth"
]

Direct typed receiving contexts can give raw numeric literals an exact type.
Later direct exact uses in the same function body can also constrain a retained
numeric literal origin. Copies and writes connect to that same origin. This
doesn't infer arbitrary values from later uses or work backwards through named
arithmetic.

Without an exact constraint, whole-number literals default to `I32` and
decimal/exponent literals default to `F64`. See
@../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for the receiving contexts and
@../numbers (Numbers) for the numeric types.

The core types include:

- `Bool` for `true` and `false`
- the exact signed and unsigned integer types
- the exact binary-float types
- `Char` for one character
- `String` for text

Collections, structs, choices and options build larger values from these ideas.
